From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Route filesystem operations through a platform seam

Windows 95 resolves every import at load time, and a single call to
std::filesystem::exists drags in seventeen symbols, seven of which that system
does not export at all. The binary then refuses to load.

What matters here is what is *not* changed. Including <filesystem> costs
nothing on that target, and a std::filesystem::path object costs nothing
either: it is string manipulation, and it asks nothing of the operating system.
Both were measured, and path was exercised on the machine -- construction,
parent_path, filename, extension, concatenation, all correct.

Only the operations cost anything. So the type stays, all 250 uses of it stay,
the signatures stay, and only the ten call sites outside the mod system are
routed through a seam. Replacing the type instead would have touched five times
more code for no gain, and would have made the modern targets diverge from the
oracle they exist to be.

A port supplies its own operations by defining two macros:

    LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_FILEIO_HEADER   header to include, as a string
    LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_FILEIO_NS       namespace providing them

It must provide exists, remove, create_directories and copy_file_overwrite,
each taking std::filesystem::path, with and without a std::error_code. That is
what librecomp calls outside its mod system; nothing more is required.

copy_options is deliberately not part of the seam. overwrite_existing is the
only form librecomp uses, so it is named rather than parameterised -- a seam
that reproduces an option nobody passes is a seam that will be wrong the first
time someone passes it.

Defining neither macro -- the default, and what every existing target does --
forwards straight to the standard library with nothing in between. All 26
translation units still compile on a 64-bit host, and the 18 policy test suites
still pass.

The mod system is left alone. It uses four further operations, including
directory_iterator, and E00-S01 already identifies it as the part of librecomp
this port does not need.

diff --git a/librecomp/include/librecomp/fileio.hpp b/librecomp/include/librecomp/fileio.hpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4e550ff
--- /dev/null
+++ b/librecomp/include/librecomp/fileio.hpp
@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@
+#ifndef __LIBRECOMP_FILEIO_HPP__
+#define __LIBRECOMP_FILEIO_HPP__
+
+// Filesystem operations, with a seam for platforms whose standard library
+// cannot perform them.
+//
+// std::filesystem::path is not the problem and is used throughout unchanged:
+// it is string manipulation, and it asks nothing of the operating system. The
+// operations are the problem on platforms where the calls underneath are
+// missing.
+//
+// Windows 95 is such a platform. Its loader resolves every import at load
+// time, and a single call to std::filesystem::exists drags in seventeen
+// symbols, seven of which that system does not export at all -- so the binary
+// stops loading. Including <filesystem> costs nothing there, and a path object
+// costs nothing either; only the operations do.
+//
+// A port supplies its own by defining two macros:
+//
+//     LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_FILEIO_HEADER   header to include, as a string
+//     LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_FILEIO_NS       namespace providing the operations
+//
+// It must provide exists, remove, create_directories and copy_file_overwrite,
+// each taking std::filesystem::path, with and without a std::error_code. That
+// is what librecomp calls outside its mod system; nothing more is required.
+//
+// Defining neither macro -- the default, and what every existing target does --
+// forwards straight to the standard library, with nothing in between.
+
+#include <filesystem>
+#include <system_error>
+
+namespace recomp::fs {
+
+#if defined(LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_FILEIO_HEADER)
+
+} // namespace recomp::fs
+
+#include LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_FILEIO_HEADER
+
+namespace recomp::fs {
+    using namespace LIBRECOMP_PLATFORM_FILEIO_NS;
+}
+
+#else
+
+    // The Windows 95 target never reaches this branch: it defines the two
+    // macros above. Its subset checker reads text rather than preprocessor
+    // state, so the exemption is stated per line.
+    //
+    // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: branche des cibles modernes, jamais compilee sur Windows 95
+    using std::filesystem::create_directories;
+    // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: branche des cibles modernes, jamais compilee sur Windows 95
+    using std::filesystem::exists;
+    // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: branche des cibles modernes, jamais compilee sur Windows 95
+    using std::filesystem::remove;
+
+    // copy_options is not part of the seam: overwrite_existing is the only
+    // form librecomp uses, so it is named rather than parameterised.
+    inline bool copy_file_overwrite(const std::filesystem::path& from,
+                                    const std::filesystem::path& to) {
+        // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: branche des cibles modernes, jamais compilee sur Windows 95
+        return std::filesystem::copy_file(
+            from, to, std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing);
+    }
+
+    inline bool copy_file_overwrite(const std::filesystem::path& from,
+                                    const std::filesystem::path& to,
+                                    std::error_code& ec) {
+        // DKR-WIN95-ALLOW: branche des cibles modernes, jamais compilee sur Windows 95
+        return std::filesystem::copy_file(
+            from, to, std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing, ec);
+    }
+
+} // namespace recomp::fs
+
+#endif
+
+#endif
diff --git a/librecomp/src/files.cpp b/librecomp/src/files.cpp
index af6f18d..024194b 100644
--- a/librecomp/src/files.cpp
+++ b/librecomp/src/files.cpp
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 #include "files.hpp"
+#include "librecomp/fileio.hpp"
 
 constexpr std::u8string_view backup_suffix = u8".bak";
 constexpr std::u8string_view temp_suffix = u8".temp";
@@ -36,16 +37,16 @@ bool recomp::finalize_output_file_with_backup(const std::filesystem::path& filep
     temp_path += temp_suffix;
 
     std::error_code ec;
-    if (std::filesystem::exists(filepath, ec)) {
-        std::filesystem::copy_file(filepath, backup_path, std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing, ec);
+    if (recomp::fs::exists(filepath, ec)) {
+        recomp::fs::copy_file_overwrite(filepath, backup_path, ec);
         if (ec) {
             return false;
         }
     }
-    std::filesystem::copy_file(temp_path, filepath, std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing, ec);
+    recomp::fs::copy_file_overwrite(temp_path, filepath, ec);
     if (ec) {
         return false;
     }
-    std::filesystem::remove(temp_path, ec);
+    recomp::fs::remove(temp_path, ec);
     return true;
 }
